          {
            "id": 923,
            "sort": 2,
            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-OOpRdCIzioTkvLx4hlPlMiUogWV\">Overall, while spending on leisure activities, transport and housing saw considerable increases over the 50-year period, the percentages for most other categories, particularly food, declined significantly. Additionally, leisure became the highest spending category in 2018.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 16220,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 924,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-O2gWdtSacosuNfxPnjIllPDLg2d\">Looking at the categories that experienced growth, leisure activities, transport and housing accounted for similar proportions of weekly spending in 1968, ranging from 7% to 10%. By 2018, leisure activities had seen the most substantial rise to 22%, becoming the highest expenditure category. Housing costs also doubled to 20%, while transport spending increased more moderately to 12%.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 16220,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 925,
            "sort": 4,
            "name": "Body 2",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-QWNpdJWlCov7ZPxD3MilEju5gue\">Regarding the remaining categories, food clearly dominated household expenditure in 1968 at 35%, while personal goods, clothing and footwear, and fuel and power each made up around 8-10% of weekly spending. However, by 2018, food costs had fallen dramatically to just 15%, and the other three categories had also declined to between 3-4% each. Meanwhile, spending on household goods, which stood at 7% in 1968, showed little change over the 50-year period.</div>\n</div>",
